id: rule-skip-stronger
title: Stronger precondition
topic: Skip
example-of: SKIP
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule SKIP to decide whether this triple can be derived.

pre: x > 5
post: x > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  skip       // s
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the precondition given by the rule SKIP
rule: SKIP
focus: s
text:
  The rule SKIP gives the triple whose precondition is the postcondition:
  `skip` does not change the state.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = Q           // wp(skip, Q) = Q
  = x > 0       // Q is x > 0
set: R1 = x > 0

step: P implies R1, with the consequence rule
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  The precondition $P$ is not the formula $R1$, so the rule SKIP alone
  does not give the triple. The consequence rule gives it if $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: x > 5 ==> x > 0
why:
  Every integer greater than 5 is greater than 0. So the triple is valid,
  but it needs the consequence rule as well as SKIP.
